# CoRe Cycle (short for Code-Review Cycle)

Use this skill to run a coding task through two explicit roles:

- Agent A implements the task.
- Agent B reviews the result and reports only actionable issues in a structured severity-based format.
- Agent A fixes reported issues and returns the work for another review round.

## Start With Config

Use this lookup order before delegating work:

1. If a project config exists at `.core-cycle/config.json`, use it.
2. Otherwise, if a user-level default config exists, tell the user which file was found and ask whether to use it for this project or reconfigure now.
3. Otherwise, use the bundled default config at `config/default.json` in this skill.

If only a user-level config or the bundled default exists, you may use it directly for the current run or copy it into `.core-cycle/config.json` and edit it manually. Do not require `scripts/pair_config.py` just to bootstrap from the checked-in default.

If the user asks to configure or reconfigure the pair at any time, repeat the configuration step even if a config already exists.

## Configure The Agent Pair

When no project config exists, or when the user asks to reconfigure:

1. Show searchable options for tools and models.
2. For Agent A, ask whether to run in the current harness, use a sub-agent in the current harness, or use an external coding harness.
3. For Agent B, ask whether to run in the current harness, use a sub-agent in the current harness, or use an external review harness.
4. Ask the user which implementation setup to use for Agent A.
5. Ask the user which review setup to use for Agent B.
6. Ask whether Agent A should create a git commit after each implementation or fix step.
7. If commits are enabled, ask for the preferred commit message template. The commit message template should extend other required parts of the git commit message. For example, if the usage of a ticketing system requires you to include the ticket number in the commit message, this part should not be removed.
8. Ask whether all intermediate commits should be squashed into a single final commit when Agent B approves. Squashing keeps the working history clean while preserving full traceability during the review cycle.
9. If squash is enabled, ask whether to squash **only unpushed commits** (recommended: yes — avoids rewriting commits already on `origin` and the need for force-push).
10. If squash is enabled, ask for the squash commit message template. This template is used for the single final commit that replaces all intermediate ones. It supports the same placeholders as the step template plus `{task_id}` and `{summary}`. Remind the user that the body of the squash commit is synthesized by Agent A from the task description and resolved review issues — only the first-line template needs to be configured.
11. Save the chosen settings into the project config file.

Use the helper for discovery:

```bash
python3 scripts/pair_config.py catalog
python3 scripts/pair_config.py catalog --kind tool
python3 scripts/pair_config.py catalog --kind model
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search current
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search subagent --kind tool
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search codex
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search copilot
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search opencode
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search pi
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search review --kind tool
python3 scripts/pair_config.py search gpt --kind model
```

Treat the catalog as a convenience shortlist, not an authoritative registry. Custom runners, commands, and model IDs are always allowed when the user specifies them.

Use these runner rules:

- `current-harness`: Run the role directly in the current harness without spawning a sub-agent. Prefer model `current` here.
- `current-harness-subagent`: Stay inside the current harness and spawn a fresh worker/reviewer with the configured model.
- `current` as a model means reuse the current harness configured model instead of selecting an explicit model ID.
- External runners like `codex`, `claude`, `gemini`, `copilot`, `opencode`, `pi`, or `custom` launch a separate coding harness or CLI and pass the request through the configured command template.
- Do not rely on changing the model of an already-running agent mid-task.
- Do not treat a backgrounded step in the same agent as equivalent to an independent reviewer.
- For standard external runners `codex`, `claude`, `gemini`, `copilot`, `opencode`, and `pi`, the skill provides built-in command templates. Do not ask the user to write them manually. Only `custom` should normally need a user-supplied `command_template`.
- The built-in templates call `scripts/run_external_agent.py`, which accepts a normalized interface with `{prompt}`, `{system_prompt}`, `{model}`, `{thinking_level}`, `{model_parameters_json}`, and `{output_file}` and maps those values into each CLI's real flags. The wrapper also accepts `--prompt-file` and `--system-prompt-file`; prefer those file-based flags for long, generated, or multiline prompts instead of passing large prompt text directly on the command line.

When you need a project-local config without using the helper, copy `config/default.json` from this skill into `.core-cycle/config.json` and edit the fields to match the chosen setup. Use this shape:

```json
{
